WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:02.736
-Our next guest is a talented
actor, you know,

00:00:02.736 --> 00:00:06.072
from shows such as "Mad Men,"
"Zoo," and HBO's "Watchmen."

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:08.841
He stars in "Ordinary Joe,"
which airs Mondays at 10 p.m.

00:00:08.841 --> 00:00:09.876
right here on NBC.

00:00:09.876 --> 00:00:13.480
Let's take a look.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.815
-Great show.
-Yeah, I think I'm going to go

00:00:15.815 --> 00:00:17.049
give him another encore, huh?

00:00:17.049 --> 00:00:19.586
-Maybe save your energy
for tomorrow night.

00:00:19.586 --> 00:00:21.254
-Drink this.
-What's this.

00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:23.189
-The organic equivalent
of 18 Gatorades,

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:24.724
with a little
bit of honey for your vocal.

00:00:24.724 --> 00:00:26.592
-Mm.
-And a shot of whiskey for fun.

00:00:26.592 --> 00:00:30.597
-Or you could just not blowout
your vocal cords.

00:00:30.597 --> 00:00:34.500
-Oh, that's good. I love G.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:37.470
Oh, I'm so happy that you're
here. Oh.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:41.774
-please, welcome to the show,
James Wolk. How are you, James?

00:00:41.774 --> 00:00:44.944
-I'm good. I'm good.
How are you, sir?

00:00:44.944 --> 00:00:46.579
-I'm doing well.
I'm hanging in there.

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:48.581
I'm very --
We've never met before.

00:00:48.581 --> 00:00:51.817
I'm a huge fan of your work,
and I'm also someone

00:00:51.817 --> 00:00:53.720
I feel like has been living
a parallel existence.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.589
You have a 5-year-old
and a 3-year-old.

00:00:56.589 --> 00:00:58.391
I have one of each of those
as well.

00:00:58.391 --> 00:01:00.760
How have you survived
through the pandemic?

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:04.029
-Oh my. Yeah, we have a
5-year-old boy, Charlie,

00:01:04.029 --> 00:01:06.965
and our daughter's
two and a half Lucy Kate.

00:01:06.965 --> 00:01:11.637
And it's been like,
it's been a while. It's been --

00:01:11.637 --> 00:01:14.273
It's been amazing to have them
to keep us busy

00:01:14.273 --> 00:01:16.809
and keep our mind busy
with positive things.

00:01:16.809 --> 00:01:18.744
But it's also like being
locked up in the house

00:01:18.744 --> 00:01:21.881
with like two lunatics,
24 hours a day.

00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:24.350
I don't know
how your experience was.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:25.851
-You know what we did, we added.

00:01:25.851 --> 00:01:28.221
We decided to have another baby
and just it solved everything.

00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:30.924
Everybody is super chill now, so
just can't recommend it enough.

00:01:30.924 --> 00:01:32.859
-We were thinking about
doing that.

00:01:32.859 --> 00:01:34.193
That's exactly
what we were thinking.

00:01:34.193 --> 00:01:36.863
-It's a real or real cure-all.

00:01:36.863 --> 00:01:38.565
So.
-Yeah.

00:01:38.565 --> 00:01:41.700
-I think the thing that a lot of
people did during the pandemic

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.570
is they went back, they
revisited old shows they loved.

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:48.440
My wife and I, we just finished
a rewatch of "Mad Men."

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:51.477
So Bob Benson, that's the first
time I ever laid eyes on you.

00:01:51.477 --> 00:01:53.846
And I enjoyed it so much more
even in the rewatch,

00:01:53.846 --> 00:01:56.315
because it was such a mysterious
character.

00:01:56.315 --> 00:01:58.084
You sort of get caught off guard
by him

00:01:58.084 --> 00:01:59.552
the first time you watched it.

00:01:59.552 --> 00:02:01.420
What was it like
stepping into a show like that

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:05.257
so late into the run
and having such a giant impact?

00:02:05.257 --> 00:02:07.593
-Yeah, well,
it was it was really interesting

00:02:07.593 --> 00:02:10.797
because I came in in Season 6,

00:02:10.797 --> 00:02:12.698
which was the second-to-last
season.

00:02:12.698 --> 00:02:15.734
I was on Season 6 and 7
and I was a fan of the show.

00:02:15.734 --> 00:02:17.470
Loved the show.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:19.538
And when I came in, all I knew
was I was playing

00:02:19.538 --> 00:02:20.573
this guy named Bob,

00:02:20.573 --> 00:02:22.842
he's a new guy in the office.

00:02:22.842 --> 00:02:26.345
And the way that Matt Weiner,
the creator, worked

00:02:26.345 --> 00:02:30.650
is I would only get the script
for the upcoming episode

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:32.018
the week before.

00:02:32.018 --> 00:02:35.822
So I was finding out a lot
about Bob as the series went on.

00:02:35.822 --> 00:02:37.757
And so there were so many times
when I read the script

00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:40.693
and I was like, "What? He's
speaking fluent Spanish now?"

00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:42.895
"What's going on here
with this guy?"

00:02:42.895 --> 00:02:45.764
And it actually, as an actor,
it helps a lot

00:02:45.764 --> 00:02:47.533
because it didn't allow me
to get ahead of the role

00:02:47.533 --> 00:02:49.368
and it allowed me to
kind of play

00:02:49.368 --> 00:02:50.769
what I thought he would become.

00:02:50.769 --> 00:02:53.572
I kind of had to just stay
in the moment.

00:02:53.572 --> 00:02:55.408
But he was he was like an
enigma.

00:02:55.408 --> 00:02:56.976
And to me,
as I read each script,

00:02:56.976 --> 00:02:58.544
I was just surprised

00:02:58.544 --> 00:03:01.481
and it was kind of a cool way
to keep me on my toes.

00:03:01.481 --> 00:03:05.217
-It also has one of the most
meme-able moments

00:03:05.217 --> 00:03:07.987
in the show's history,
which was --

00:03:07.987 --> 00:03:10.656
we-- let's show the clip
for anyone who has

00:03:10.656 --> 00:03:12.358
made the mistake of
not watching "Mad Men."

00:03:12.358 --> 00:03:13.359
Let's take a look.

00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:16.295
-Bob!

00:03:16.295 --> 00:03:20.333
-How are you?
-Not great, Bob.

00:03:20.333 --> 00:03:21.734
-Not great, Bob.

00:03:21.734 --> 00:03:24.737
I think about it probably at
least two or three times a week.

00:03:24.737 --> 00:03:27.406
I think "Not great, Bob."
about my current situation.

00:03:27.406 --> 00:03:29.742
Do people ever say it to you?

00:03:29.742 --> 00:03:32.245
-It's crazy because
that has become

00:03:32.245 --> 00:03:34.480
something that my friends
will say back to me now.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:37.149
Like just they,
it's become such a meme.

00:03:37.149 --> 00:03:39.451
One of my one of my really dear
friends from New York,

00:03:39.451 --> 00:03:41.954
this is a few years ago,
right before the pandemic,

00:03:41.954 --> 00:03:44.256
he came out to L.A.,

00:03:44.256 --> 00:03:45.457
came out to Santa Monica

00:03:45.457 --> 00:03:47.060
and came out with his two sons.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:48.160
I'm like, I'm going to
take you guys

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:49.662
on an amazing bike ride
on the beach.

00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:50.730
You know, you're going
to love that.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:52.698
So we go on this bike ride.

00:03:52.698 --> 00:03:54.867
And one of his sons at the time
was like young,

00:03:54.867 --> 00:03:57.336
like three or four, and he
really had to go to the bathroom

00:03:57.336 --> 00:03:58.905
"Dad, I have to go,
I have to go."

00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:01.207
And so I was like,
"Well, Jay, there's a bathroom

00:04:01.207 --> 00:04:02.808
right over there,
you could use the bathrooms

00:04:02.808 --> 00:04:05.010
down on the beach, which are
fine.

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:07.680
You know, they're public
bathrooms, which are fine.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:09.248
And all of a sudden it
from the bathroom.

00:04:09.248 --> 00:04:12.384
I hear Jay go, like,
"Don't touch that!"

00:04:12.384 --> 00:04:13.986
"Get off the ground.
What are you doing?"

00:04:13.986 --> 00:04:16.622
"No!" I literally hear him go,
"No!" like that.

00:04:16.622 --> 00:04:21.026
And they come out and without
even thinking about it, I go.

00:04:21.026 --> 00:04:22.594
"How did everything go?"

00:04:22.594 --> 00:04:26.165
And he looks at me and he goes,
"Not great, Bob!"

00:04:26.165 --> 00:04:28.801
Just like, didn't even,
he used it back on me.

00:04:28.801 --> 00:04:30.135
And it was it was really funny.

00:04:30.135 --> 00:04:32.304
I actually was dying, laughing
because I hadn't been

00:04:32.304 --> 00:04:34.006
pulled on me like that before.

00:04:34.006 --> 00:04:36.542
-You, you also had
a very nice turn, a villainous

00:04:36.542 --> 00:04:38.878
turn on the "Watchmen,"

00:04:38.878 --> 00:04:41.247
which was, I feel like
the last water cooler show

00:04:41.247 --> 00:04:42.748
before it was no longer healthy
to stand

00:04:42.748 --> 00:04:44.417
around the water cooler.

00:04:44.417 --> 00:04:47.286
It was that I think
that was another show.

00:04:47.286 --> 00:04:49.789
Is it safe to say that
you were probably in the dark

00:04:49.789 --> 00:04:52.291
over the course of the series,
knowing where your

00:04:52.291 --> 00:04:54.827
character's arc was going to
land you?

00:04:54.827 --> 00:04:55.928
-I feel like
that's the thing now,

00:04:55.928 --> 00:04:57.629
is I just get cast
and then no one tells me

00:04:57.629 --> 00:05:00.532
what's going to happen
to the characters.

00:05:00.532 --> 00:05:02.868
That shows that it was actually
something very similar.

00:05:02.868 --> 00:05:07.106
I knew I was familiar with the
graphic novel "Watchmen,"

00:05:07.106 --> 00:05:08.674
but Damon Lindelof,
the creator,

00:05:08.674 --> 00:05:11.243
was very clear that this was
going to be like a remake,

00:05:11.243 --> 00:05:15.647
kind of like a retake on it --
and not a remake a retake.

00:05:15.647 --> 00:05:18.884
And so this character, Joe Keene
that I was playing

00:05:18.884 --> 00:05:21.386
had not been
in any other graphic novels,

00:05:21.386 --> 00:05:24.023
and all I knew was that there
was something that wasn't

00:05:24.023 --> 00:05:26.459
quite what it seemed with him.

00:05:26.459 --> 00:05:29.261
But I had no idea that
by the end of the series,

00:05:29.261 --> 00:05:31.530
Can I give a spoiler
if people haven't watched it?

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:33.332
Should I say what happens
at the end or not?

00:05:33.332 --> 00:05:35.968
-I feel like enough time has
passed and we have to

00:05:35.968 --> 00:05:37.769
punish people
who can't watch shows on time.

00:05:37.769 --> 00:05:41.139
I mean, people have had a full
pandemic to catch up.

00:05:41.139 --> 00:05:44.544
-Right? Like two years in a
pandemic. So thank you.

00:05:44.544 --> 00:05:46.845
So I had no idea that the end
you'd be wearing

00:05:46.845 --> 00:05:50.750
just like a black underwear
and explode

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:53.152
into smithereens
in this big machine.

00:05:53.152 --> 00:05:55.988
But I remember Damon called me,
he was like, he was like,

00:05:55.988 --> 00:05:57.322
"You're doing such great work
in the show.

00:05:57.322 --> 00:06:00.559
But I just have to say
to some characters have to die

00:06:00.559 --> 00:06:02.161
and he needs to die."

00:06:02.161 --> 00:06:03.929
And I totally understood why.

00:06:03.929 --> 00:06:06.331
But what a way to go was,
it was the finale.

00:06:06.331 --> 00:06:09.869
-It was really, a really great
way to go.

00:06:09.869 --> 00:06:14.139
And I guess when you have roles
like Bob Benson or Keene,

00:06:14.139 --> 00:06:17.576
it's tough to find a role
that that ticks all those boxes.

00:06:17.576 --> 00:06:20.313
And so you went out and you got
a show where you play

00:06:20.313 --> 00:06:21.514
three different characters.

00:06:21.514 --> 00:06:22.949
Can you,
for anybody who's missed it,

00:06:22.949 --> 00:06:26.452
can you just explain the premise
real quick for "Ordinary Joe"?

00:06:26.452 --> 00:06:27.820
-Yeah, absolutely.

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:31.423
So "Ordinary Joe" is basically
it plays with this idea

00:06:31.423 --> 00:06:34.226
of what if and how the choices
that we make in our lives

00:06:34.226 --> 00:06:37.630
really shaped who we become
and what we become.

00:06:37.630 --> 00:06:41.533
And so it plays with the three
iterations of this guy's life.

00:06:41.533 --> 00:06:43.836
You see the decision that
he makes right after college

00:06:43.836 --> 00:06:46.538
that sends him down
three totally different paths,

00:06:46.538 --> 00:06:48.674
depending on what decision
he made that day.

00:06:48.674 --> 00:06:51.577
So it's kind of like a choose
your own adventure novel,

00:06:51.577 --> 00:06:53.579
except you're watching
the series

00:06:53.579 --> 00:06:55.347
and you get
to see how his life plays out,

00:06:55.347 --> 00:06:57.416
and it's drastically different
in all three lives.

00:06:57.416 --> 00:07:00.653
Like in the clip we just saw, he
becomes an incredibly selfish,

00:07:00.653 --> 00:07:04.290
very famous recording artist,
musician.

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:07.593
And in another life,
he ends up having a kid

00:07:07.593 --> 00:07:09.995
really early on
with his college sweetheart

00:07:09.995 --> 00:07:11.197
and becomes a really young dad.

00:07:11.197 --> 00:07:14.199
And he's
a totally different person.

00:07:14.199 --> 00:07:17.036
And so you get this to see
how our situations

00:07:17.036 --> 00:07:18.737
and the choices we make
really shape us.

00:07:18.737 --> 00:07:20.873
And the show's
really fun in that way.

00:07:20.873 --> 00:07:23.643
-One thread that ties
all the timelines together

00:07:23.643 --> 00:07:26.445
is the connection to Billy Joel.

00:07:26.445 --> 00:07:29.282
I'm assuming people who work
on this show must have a deep

00:07:29.282 --> 00:07:32.818
abiding love for Mr. Joel
and his work.

00:07:32.818 --> 00:07:36.422
-It is a unwavering love
for Mr. Joel.

00:07:36.422 --> 00:07:38.957
Russell Friend,
one of our creators --

00:07:38.957 --> 00:07:40.226
The two creators are
Garrett Lerner

00:07:40.226 --> 00:07:42.861
and Russell Friend,
Russell is from Long Island.

00:07:42.861 --> 00:07:45.097
He's from the Oyster Bay Area.

00:07:45.097 --> 00:07:47.599
And what I've come to learn --
as I'm from the Midwest,

00:07:47.599 --> 00:07:51.236
I'm from Michigan, so this is
this is all new to me --

00:07:51.236 --> 00:07:52.304
that if you're from Oyster Bay,

00:07:52.304 --> 00:07:54.640
you are like
a diehard Billy Joel guy.

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:57.776
So that's where the whole
Billy Joel throughline

00:07:57.776 --> 00:08:00.479
of the of the show comes from.

00:08:00.479 --> 00:08:02.281
-Well, there's got to be
something to tie

00:08:02.281 --> 00:08:03.349
in three timelines together.

00:08:03.349 --> 00:08:05.451
Let's let it be Billy Joel.

00:08:05.451 --> 00:08:06.986
And congratulations
on the show.

00:08:06.986 --> 00:08:08.988
I'm so glad we finally got a
chance to meet.

00:08:08.988 --> 00:08:11.290
Let's do it in person next time.

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:12.725
-Thank you. Same with me, Seth.

00:08:12.725 --> 00:08:14.760
Thanks a lot.
-James Wolk, everybody.

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:16.762
"Ordinary Joe"
airs Monday nights at 10 p.m.

00:08:16.762 --> 00:08:18.030
here on NBC,
we'll be right back.

